is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: sshswitch
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the L2/L3 bridge configuration on sshswitch, which provides the default gateways 10.5.0.254 (VLAN 5) and 10.6.0.254 (VLAN 6). Mechanism: After a reload, sshswitch is bridging the physical trunk to the access switch (eth0) together with the bond to the core (bond0) in br_v1 (mgmt), while simultaneously bridging VLAN subinterfaces eth0.5/eth0.6 with bond0.5/bond0.6 in br_v5/br_v6. With STP disabled, this creates parallel L2 paths for the same tagged frames (via br_v1 and via br_v5/br_v6), leading to L2 loops/MAC flapping and intermittent ARP failure, which presents to users as “can’t reach gateway” on both 10.5.0.x and 10.6.0.x at the same time. Localization: On sshswitch: brctl/bridge show proves br_v1 includes bond0 and eth0, and br_v5/br_v6 each include both the eth0.* and bond0.* VLAN devices; STP is off on all bridges (brctl show). The gateway IPs and MAC (42:00:b3:49:b6:0d) are on sshswitch (ifconfig shows br_v5/br_v6 have 10.5.0.254/10.6.0.254), confirming it is the gateway device. Root cause: eth0 was mistakenly enslaved to br_v1 and bond0.5/.6 were added to br_v5/br_v6, creating an internal L2 loop across the trunk and bond with STP disabled. Corrective fix: Remove eth0 from br_v1 so it only carries VLAN subifs to br_v5/br_v6; remove bond0.5 and bond0.6 from br_v5/br_v6 (or don’t create them); optionally enable bridge VLAN filtering and/or STP to prevent future loops. This restores a single, loop-free path for VLANs 5 and 6 to their gateways. === END ===